{
  "summary": "The existing ideation has thoroughly explored terminal_stage vs active_phases, but both proposals overlook a simpler structural observation: the pipeline already has natural 'seams' at exactly the stage-check boundaries in _run_pipeline_impl (lines 1564, 1657, 1694), and the routing after each block already converges to well-defined handoff stages (reviewing-plan -> approved, spec-review -> implementing, review-impl -> final-human-approval). Rather than adding a new config field that the pipeline checks at runtime, the more robust approach is to make the pipeline entry point itself board-aware — a board with terminal_stage='plan' would simply have its items enter _run_pipeline_impl normally but return after the plan review block routes to final-human-approval instead of approved. This means the terminal_stage check is a single conditional at exactly two points: (1) after plan review PASS (line 1600-1608), redirect to final-human-approval instead of approved; (2) after spec review PASS (line 1671-1676), redirect to final-human-approval instead of implementing. The item never reaches a block it shouldn't — no skip logic, no gating, no data contract violations. The config field should be called 'terminal_phase' (not 'terminal_stage' — stages are filesystem directories, phases are pipeline execution blocks) with valid values 'plan', 'spec', or 'impl' (default), mapping to which block is the last one that runs before routing to final-human-approval. This is a two-line change at each block boundary plus one config field, totaling under 15 lines of new code."
}
